---
/*
 * The TypeScript logo, in the landing page's lead.
 *
 * It replaced a ⚡. The bolt said "fast" beside a clause that is not about
 * speed — "Pure TypeScript" is a claim about what the package contains, and
 * the language's own mark states it directly where an emoji only decorated it.
 *
 * WHICH LOGO, which is the reason this file was rewritten.
 * <https://www.typescriptlang.org/branding/> publishes three: a primary mark,
 * an "alternative" described as the "single color variant which has the 'TS'
 * cut out, useful for when you need a single color design", and a lettermark.
 * Of the primary it says, in as many words: "The 'TS' in the logo is white, not
 * transparent by default."
 *
 * The first version here drew simple-icons' `typescript` path, which is a
 * faithful copy of the cut-out variant, and then filled it with the primary
 * blue. That is neither logo. The letters were a hole, so they showed whatever
 * was behind them: on the light canvas cream, near enough to white to pass for
 * the real thing, and on the dark canvas #0f0e0b — a blue tile with two
 * near-black letters in it, which is not a color the palette contains and not
 * a logo TypeScript publishes. Two themes, and the mark was only right in one
 * of them, by accident.
 *
 * So the artwork is the primary logo, copied out of `ts-logo-128.svg` in the
 * asset pack that page links: a #3178c6 rect and a white letterform path, both
 * verbatim. Verbatim matters more here than anywhere else on this site, because
 * "modify the shape of the logos when used" is on that page's Please Don't
 * list. That one line rules out redrawing the letters, and it also rules out
 * the tidying an engineer reaches for by reflex — repointing the fill at a
 * token, merging the rect into the path, rounding the corner radius to taste.
 *
 * The 128 rendition rather than the 512, because the corner radius is not
 * constant across the official files: 4.69% of the side at 128, 7.81% at 256,
 * 9.77% at 512. Each is drawn for the size it is named for. This one renders at
 * about 18px.
 *
 * The rest of that Please Don't list is why this use is allowed at all. The
 * logo is not our application's logo, it is not integrated into our logo, and
 * nothing here is named to imply TypeScript's endorsement. It sits beside the
 * words "Pure TypeScript" and names the language the SDK is written in, which
 * is nominative use. TypeScript is a trademark of Microsoft Corporation;
 * Microsoft is named nowhere on this site as a partner or an endorser.
 *
 * Not a link, and that is deliberate rather than an oversight. `OsiMark.astro`
 * has to link to opensource.org because its license requires it; nothing
 * requires this one, and a second outbound link above the fold would be a
 * second thing competing with the one primary action. It is aria-hidden for
 * the same reason the battery is: the words beside it already say TypeScript,
 * so announcing the mark would only repeat them.
 *
 * Sized in `global.css` rather than here — see `.ts-mark`, which carries why
 * this one is not the same size as the keyhole, and why that number changed
 * when the artwork did.
 */
---

<span class="ts-mark">
  <svg viewBox="0 0 128 128" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false">
    <rect width="128" height="128" rx="6" fill="#3178c6"></rect>
